WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:04.940
[Tone]

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:23.500
[Background music]

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.800
This is looking south on Branch Road about 10:29 AM.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.620
You can see the funnel touching down on the outskirts of town.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.680
Here's the monster still moving northeast about 10:31, just two minutes later.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.500
That's one of our ambulances on its way to pick up casualties.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:49.080
This is looking west on Fourth Street about 10:42.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.160
There are people under that funnel.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:01:01.300
[Background music]

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.380
This is one of the emergency vehicles.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.840
They're following the path of destruction and trying to keep track of the funnel's direction.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:15.760
[Sirens]

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:17.640
There's Simmons Chemical.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.060
Here we had most of our casualties.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.660
By 11 o'clock the list was taken.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.520
We had four fire companies here, and our rescue units.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.760
The men in white suits are civil defense rescue teams.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:43.100
[Sirens in distance]

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.160
We had every available fire company here.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.840
That's the mayor.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.140
He's making a quick survey of damage to the city.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.660
He's trying to determine whether or not to ask the governor to declare the area a disaster area

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.180
so we can get more help.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.440
[Woman yelling incoherently.]

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.780
You see women trying to find out if their husbands are among the dead.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.740
We were lucky to have the facilities of a snorkel to get into the smoldering embers

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:19.400
and make sure the fire was dead out.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.500
This is the explosion area where wind knocked over a tank,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.720
and it blew up,

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.380
adding that much more havoc to that caused by the funnel.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:37.140
Some of the casualties needed immediate care at the explosion site.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.220
[Voice over a public address system is giving instructions.]

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:46.660
Here you see rescue workers trying to get people out from under that debris.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:55.000
Despite the quick response of firefighters and rescue teams, we needed help,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.980
and we were lucky that so many plant workers kept their heads, stayed and helped with the rescue work.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.900
Here again women trying to get to the entrance.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.060
There were many killed.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.860
It was a sorrowful time for those who arrived at the scene

00:03:17.860 --> 00:03:23.080
and found that a member of their family had been a victim of the disaster.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.000
We didn't know how many more casualties would be under the debris.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.740
In another part of the plant which had sustained only wind damage,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:40.400
we set up a first-aid station where ambulances and other vehicles could get to the casualties,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.900
get in, and get them out to a hospital if necessary.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:50.160
Spectators and relatives trying to get to and find their loved ones caused a continuing traffic problem,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.380
and there was no getting them out of the way.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.040
There's Dr. Ryan.

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.120
He was everywhere, tending to the injured.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.600
Wherever we went, the ever-present problem of spectators trying to find members of their family.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:15.740
[Sirens]

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.960
We were making use of any vehicle we could get our hands on,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.180
even private automobiles.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:25.820
The injured,

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.160
the dead,

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.500
the rescuers.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:34.100
The frantic effort to retrieve life, to preserve it.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:40.440
We respond almost by instinct, with total maximum effort.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:43.800
There's no holding back.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.580
In an emergency like this you give everything you've got.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.720
It's the price you pay.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:56.780
[Background music]

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:59.540
Memorial Hospital, the city's largest.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.520
At 11:15 this Thursday morning,

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:06.700
the staff is attempting to provide a quality standard of care for all casualties,

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:11.020
and their hospital is damaged, without city light or power.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:12.380
[Sirens]

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.140
[Voice on public address system:] Mr. Barnes, please report to the emergency room, Mr. Barnes.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:06:02.240
[Sirens, shouting, more words from public address system]

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.700
[Narrator:] Richard Barnes, administrator at Memorial.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:10.180
He has to be everywhere at once this morning as his hospital strains to meet this emergency.

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.280
[Man walking with Mr. Barnes:] What about this power problem?

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:15.620
[Richard Barnes:] They promise to turn us on in a couple of minutes.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:18.680
The generator is working but it's only emergency lighting.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.940
[Man walking with Barnes:] The elevators are still out?

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:25.220
[Richard Barnes:] Right.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:31.760
[Doctor:] Oh hi, Dick, I had them page you.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.900
You know anything yet on how many we can expect?

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.980
[Richard Barnes:] We haven't been able to reach the plant.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.320
We're getting a lot of casualties from other parts of town.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.100
[Man:] Mr. Barnes, should we use the emergency ambulances to send the discharged patients home?

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.500
[Richard Burns:] No, I want those units kept right here until we're sure all the casualties are in.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.620
Now tell the people in the lobby to telephone home or telephone a cab

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.180
and get them out of here as best as you can.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:52.260
[Man:] Yes, sir.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.320
Sir, we had five injuries right here in the hospital,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.920
cut by glass breakage.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.520
[Richard Barnes:] Well, with this patient load, how many beds are we going to need?

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:06.720
[Doctor:] Well, let's see. We had three DOAs, and one expired here.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.080
I'd say probably, oh--let's say at least 25 more anyway.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.260
We've got some pretty bad burn cases from the chemical plant.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.980
They must have had a pretty hard time up there.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:23.140
[Patient lying on gurney:] One of the tanks was damaged and it blew up.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:25.340
I was just going outside.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.120
[Doctor:] What was in the tank?

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.240
[Narrator:] Richard Barnes can't stay to find out.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.220
With casualties arriving from all over the city,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:36.500
he has to keep moving to stay on top of a fast-changing situation.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.680
Where should the ambulances be sent?

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.920
How many more patients will need care?

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:45.300
Will the supplies hold out?

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.800
Without communication, he can only guess.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.780
[Man on phone:] We're still having problems getting through.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.540
Oh, the police called.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.540
There are more casualties on the way.

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:58.080
[Richard Barnes:] Where do we stand on staff?

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.960
[Woman seated at table:] All available interns and residents are on duty

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.840
and most of the off-duty nurses have come in.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:10.360
[Man on phone:] We did talk to Garten Hospital.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.740
They've got all they can handle over there too.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.840
[Richard Barnes:] This is Thursday.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:28.840
Let's plan to discuss this communications problem and these other difficulties at the staff meeting on Monday.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.040
[Man on phone:] Wait.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:32.160
I'm getting through.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:33.080
It's the plant.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.920
Hello? Hello, this is Memorial Hospital calling.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.840
Let me talk to--hello?

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.480
This is urgent.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.560
Yes, yes, I know that, but...

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:47.880
They hung up on us.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.280
[Richard Barnes:] Keep trying.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:57.520
[Train whistle blowing.]

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.480
[Mayor:] Well, it's serious enough to warrant evacuation.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.000
[Dr. Hickman:] If they don't close off the valves,

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.880
I'll recommend we evacuate the whole east side of town.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.800
[Firefighter:] We should know something in five minutes...

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.760
[Narrator:] This is Dr. James Hickman, the city's public health officer.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:16.700
He has expert knowledge of the medical implications of this disaster.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:21.280
He will have a crucial role in averting further casualties.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.000
At the improvised aid station, the casualties continue to arrive from the explosion site.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:29.680
They are cared for and given transportation to Memorial Hospital

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.700
by ambulances, private automobiles, and station wagons.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:44.800
[Background activity, horns, and sounds]

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.360
Tom Ryan is a plastic surgeon, but this morning he's very much in general practice.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:54.200
He must care for the injured with limited professional assistance.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:58.480
Relatives of the plant workers and others pitch in and help.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.820
Fortunately, Dr. Ryan's experience on the Medical Society's Disaster Committee

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.080
has prepared him for some of the difficulties he's meeting.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.200
[Ambulance driver:] Hey, doc, Memorial is not my hospital.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:09.960
[Dr. Tom Ryan:] These patients have got to go to Memorial.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:10.960
Now you know the way, don't you?

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:11.580
[Ambulance driver:] Yes, sir.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:14.800
[Dr. Tom Ryan:] Give this envelope to the admitting physician and write this down.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:22.080
Tell him you'll be sending Memorial at least eight more bad burns, eight, and four serious traumas.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.260
Okay? Go ahead.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:35.240
[Sirens, talking, public address system sounds]

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:40.440
[Narrator:] Monday morning just after 10:00,

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:44.700
workmen were busy replacing glass in the main lobby of Memorial Hospital.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:49.220
At the same time, the regular Monday meeting of the hospital staff.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.260
[Richard Barnes:] This office commends the medical family of the city.

00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.460
We are most pleased with the outstanding performance

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.640
by the staff of Memorial Hospital in meeting this emergency.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.460
Once again, Memorial has rendered valuable service to this community.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:11.360
With regard to any suggestions you may have for improvement of emergency health services,

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:15.980
Dr. Hickman has assured me he will be happy to discuss this with you.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:21.400
With sincere appreciation, Ralph W. Lewis, Mayor.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:26.220
The mayor seems to think we're pretty good, and I agree, of course.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.200
I know that we were lucky.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.620
[African-American man:] That's true.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:34.020
If the tornado had turned north, it would have gone right through town.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:37.360
[Richard Barnes:] I mean, we were lucky.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.660
Now, we're devoting this meeting to disaster readiness,

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.040
to find out what you think of our performance last week.

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:48.220
Not just this hospital, but the whole medical response.

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:51.040
Frankly, if things had been any worse,

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.520
I'm afraid we would have been in deep trouble.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:57.800
[Man wearing glasses:] Now, Dick, you're going to have to clarify that.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:04.380
You know as well as we all know that every casualty that came in here received the best possible attention.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.260
There's no question about that.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.020
[Doctor:] The treatment was excellent.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:11.820
Whether the patients were handled in terms of immediate needs is another matter.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:12.940
We could have done better.

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:14.000
Let me finish.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:19.660
We ought to establish sorting: triage from the moment the first casualty is admitted.

00:12:19.660 --> 00:12:24.180
[Man with glasses:] We didn't need sorting; we weren't dealing in mass casualties.

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:25.660
[Richard Barnes:] Weren't we?

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.500
We had no way of knowing.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:33.240
For a good 40 minutes, we couldn't estimate how many more casualties were going to come in.

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.600
We admitted 42 in all, plus five injured here.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:40.380
It could have been 82, 100 or even 200.

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:42.720
[Man with glasses:] Why didn't we know?

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.780
Didn't we have communication with the disaster areas?

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:51.260
I mean the plant, the Fourth Street School, and the downtown area for that matter.

00:12:51.260 --> 00:12:53.240
[Dr. Tom Ryan:] I'd like to answer that.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:57.980
As disaster committee chairman, I was at one site or the other all the time.

00:12:57.980 --> 00:13:04.440
I couldn't get through to Memorial until after the last casualties had been put aboard ambulances.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:08.360
[Nurse:] Dr. Ryan, was the police two-way radio made available to you?

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:10.340
[Dr. Tom Ryan:] I found the police very cooperative.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:13.820
The problem was to find a squad car near our aid area that wasn't being used....

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.080
[Narrator:] You started something, Mr. Barnes.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:20.440
You know your staff: they're still close enough to the shock and confusion of last week

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.680
to be just a little scared at what might have happened.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:31.200
If the tornado had turned north, or if it came at night with no light, no power.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:35.160
[African-American man:] As recently as three months ago,

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:39.440
I remember we decided we couldn't afford any more generating equipment.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.300
[Richard Barnes:] That's true.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:46.860
Today I'm sure we'll decide differently, and chalk it up to the high cost of survival.

00:13:47.740 --> 00:13:54.100
I'm sure there are a great many things that can be done in this hospital to ensure a better state of readiness,

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:58.860
and evidently we've got a long way to go in coordinating the whole medical effort.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:01.440
But where do we begin?

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.220
Where do we go from here?

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:10.720
[Narrator:] The price of survival.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:13.720
Mankind has paid it over and over.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:17.220
For the sake of life we pay it willingly.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:21.420
We gather our injured and bind their wounds.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:24.580
We bury our dead.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:26.940
We rebuild.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:34.440
And next time we think we will know better how to cope with disaster.

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.180
So destructive.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:42.820
[Music]

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:45.540
So viciously unpredictable.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.040
Next time.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.860
Sometimes because we haven't planned for disaster,

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.940
the price we pay is far greater than it has to be.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.600
So much bravery deserves preparedness.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.520
But in the face of disaster, ready or not,

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:07.440
we pay the price, if we can.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.080
[Music]

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:16.180
[Mr. Barnes:] In the chronicles of disaster, our city's experience was a minor entry,

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:22.500
but it was enough to make us realize that special effort must be devoted to emergency services.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.660
Next time it might not be so easy to pick up the pieces.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:33.040
Looking at the problem citywide, the mayor and council were just as concerned as we were at the hospital.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:38.060
Right after the tornado we got two new bulldozers to speed clearance of debris.

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:44.000
At the same time, a new director for civil defense came on duty,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.720
a trained full-time administrator.

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.080
With energetic leadership,

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:53.240
the Fallout Shelter Program, including the marking and stocking of supplies

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:57.260
that we had started a couple of years back, was finally pushed to completion.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:03.820
The city began to accumulate additional stocks of emergency food supplies against natural disasters.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:09.360
At first glance, the list of emergency services seemed almost too elaborate.

00:16:10.340 --> 00:16:14.640
But even for a city the size of ours, everyone was essential.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:20.740
And that's how we three found ourselves knee-deep in disaster planning,

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:25.420
with a mandate from the mayor to get emergency health services organized.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.760
I was tapped along with Tom Ryan who was chairman of the Medical Society's Disaster Committee.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:36.820
Jim Hickman, our public health officer, has an expert knowledge of preventive medicine

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:40.480
and he works continuously with different city departments.

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:45.700
We soon began to realize that no one had ever tackled this problem before.

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:52.600
We could study existing plans but there was no ready-made formula to meet the specific needs of our community.

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:58.260
The disaster hazards we were subject to, the facilities and resources we could marshal.

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:02.040
Some work had been done.

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.600
Each of the three hospitals in the area had a disaster plan,

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:10.120
but none of us had ever considered gearing our plan to the other two.

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:15.760
We had a long way to go before we could call it a community plan.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.980
So we started with simple, basic questions...

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:22.320
so simple no one had ever asked them before.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.980
Were stretchers interchangeable between ambulances?

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:32.160
And what about the emergency equipment on our vehicles?

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.260
One point in our favor:

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:39.540
many other people were just as concerned as we were about the problem.

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:46.340
At first, wherever we went, surveying our vulnerability to disaster or examining medical facilities,

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:48.400
we had good cooperation.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:56.000
Then as memory of the tornado began to fade, with it went some of the eagerness.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:18:01.620
People would talk to us, if it wouldn't take too much time or cost any money.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:08.680
In the field of medicine and health, working with other physicians and hospital people,

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:11.040
we got a great deal accomplished quickly.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:21.680
We were dealing with colleagues, people we knew, in terms of specialized needs we both understood.

00:18:25.040 --> 00:18:29.380
We soon drew the local nurses' association into active support.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:37.480
With the assistance of the Medical Society, we soon had a record of every physician residing in the area,

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:41.680
and we set up a running inventory of the available medical supplies

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:44.900
including biologicals and pharmaceuticals.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:50.860
This included the stocks in drug and surgical supply houses.

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:57.080
We found the state health department and the Red Cross were useful sources of information and assistance.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:59.000
[Instructor at head of classroom:] It's very easy to construct,

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.840
and Chester, would you and Larry come up and help us demonstrate this method?

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:08.180
The fundamentals are, again, very easy to perform but they are...

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.360
[Mr. Barnes:] The students in this medical self-help course

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.900
are maintenance, kitchen help, office workers...the ancillary staff of the hospital.

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:19.620
[Instructor:] All right, Carolyn, if you would come up and help us demonstrate this.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:22.440
Do not be afraid that the folded blanket will fall

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:25.840
because the weight of the injured person will hold it firmly in place.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:27.980
All right.

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:28.740
Good.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:32.780
Larry, if you and Chester will pick her up now and transport her to the back of the room,

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:35.260
just like you would transport an injured person.

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.500
[Mr. Barnes:] With this basic training they can function as aides in time of disaster.

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:41.360
[Instructor:] That's very good.

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.520
Carolyn, you look like you've been hit.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:47.960
[Mr. Barnes:] We even managed to get a Packaged Disaster Hospital located in our area.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.500
[Packaged disaster hospital representative:] ...complete operating room suites by the way, in each PDH.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:55.780
Each hospital is packaged in some 660 cases such as this,

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:59.580
and they are prepositioned in every state throughout the country.

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:04.940
[Mr. Barnes:] A complete 200-bed hospital, in about 650 crates.

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:10.160
With it came a training course for physicians, nurses, and other hospital personnel.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:15.920
[Dr. Hickman:] Our disaster casualty potential far exceeds the normal bed capacity of...

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:19.900
[Mr. Barnes:] In about six weeks, we had started on a number of different activities.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.880
We had accumulated a lot of data,

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:25.700
and we were beginning to gain a basic understanding of what we'd have to work with,

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:27.960
medically-speaking, in a disaster.

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:32.300
It was when we began to reach outside the area of medicine

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:36.480
to draw on other community services, that it got more complicated.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:43.600
[Dr. Hickman:] Now listen, we've got to line up an emergency food supply for a hospital population of 600.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:48.620
That includes patients and staff for approximately eight days.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:57.980
But this does come under the city's plan for natural disasters.

00:20:57.980 --> 00:21:01.060
The mayor's office said the resources...

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:05.300
[Narrator:] They need food, transportation, a communications network.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:11.010
Without this kind of support, their emergency health plan wouldn't be worth the paper it was written on.

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:14.660
This problem of communication, to take one example.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:20.400
They agreed it should be a simple matter to link the three hospitals in the area with a disaster site.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:25.780
Then they could control the flow of casualties, manage their resources more effectively.

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:29.020
It seemed simple until they talked it over with the police department.

00:21:29.020 --> 00:21:34.340
[Police Officer 1:] Look pal, You can see how tied up the board is now, and this is just everyday routine.

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:38.340
You can imagine how busy this thing would be in an emergency like you're talking about.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:42.540
How would you go about rating one priority call over another medical call?

00:21:42.540 --> 00:21:44.480
[Police Officer 2:] Yes, and there's another problem, doc,

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:49.120
how would the police officer at the scene know which doctor is authorized to make calls?

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:54.010
[Dr. Ryan:] All right, let's take an actual emergency situation.

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:57.630
Now the police are the first ones usually to the site.

00:21:57.630 --> 00:22:01.100
[Mr. Barnes:] We learned that the municipal police department and the county sheriff's office

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:03.210
broadcast on different frequencies,

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.170
but then we were learning a lot of things we hadn't known before.

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:08.790
There was no problem we couldn't work out.

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:15.290
It took more time, more digging, and it forced us to think in terms of the obstinate realities of disaster.

00:22:15.290 --> 00:22:21.700
We reviewed our plan for the mayor's transportation advisors, another of the civil defense emergency services.

00:22:21.700 --> 00:22:26.840
Members included the biggest trucking companies in the city and the chief of the county road department.

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:31.840
[Dr. Hickman:] We must have trucks and drivers immediately post-disaster

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:39.940
to move emergency medical supplies, sanitation equipment, personnel and perhaps even casualties.

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:43.980
[Man 2:] You say here you'll need six two- and-a-half-ton trucks, and drivers.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:47.040
You're going to have to cut that figure in half at least, doc.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:52.820
[Dr. Hickman:] We've got three hospitals to take care of and we're not asking for any additional ambulances.

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:54.620
[Mr. Barnes:] That's a rock-bottom figure.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:57.180
[Man with mustache:] Every truck we can lay our hands on

00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:01.360
is already committed to emergency service of one kind or another.

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:06.800
We need trucks to clear debris, evacuate survivors, bring in food supplies.

00:23:06.800 --> 00:23:10.700
[Male:] You see, doc, you're not the only people in the disaster business.

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:18.800
[Operator:] Hello, Memorial Hospital calling.

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.460
A simulated emergency drill is underway.

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:23.600
[Richard Barnes:] We're going to need every single person we can get down here.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:24.340
[Assistant:] Yes, sir.

00:23:24.340 --> 00:23:27.940
[Richard Barnes:] And be sure that all the main arteries through the center of town are clear

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:29.820
because we're going to have emergency units going through there.

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:30.620
[Assistant:] Yes, sir.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.080
[Richard Barnes:] It's 11:04.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:37.140
Our test has been underway for 10 minutes.

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:41.640
Emergency units are now moving along the main streets towards the disaster area.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:44.480
All other traffic has been stopped.

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:47.180
[Man in vehicle:] Mayor, I just received word that one of our other ambulances

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:50.900
just passed through the square and should be here at the disaster scene any minute now.

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:51.720
[Mayor:] That's fine.

00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:55.140
Will you check back now and see whether there are any more disaster units on the way?

00:23:55.140 --> 00:23:55.800
[Man in vehicle:] Yes, sir.

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:56.350
[Mayor:] Good.

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:57.970
[Man in vehicle:] Zero, four base.

00:23:57.970 --> 00:24:00.730
[Narrator:] It's a full-scale disaster drill.

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:39.630
[Sirens and  whistles]

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:42.400
[Mr. Barnes:] For the first time, the health service plan we developed

00:24:42.410 --> 00:24:46.390
is being tested together with the other emergency services.

00:24:46.390 --> 00:24:51.770
All of that coordination we worked on so painfully is starting to pay off.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:12.720
We have our communication system working with the help of civil defense.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:16.120
This particular unit is courtesy of a local cab company,

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:20.480
and we have the police radio tied in, too, whenever we need one.

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:24.860
[Man speaking into radio:] ...we got 15 hit with flying debris and some are multiple injuries,

00:25:24.860 --> 00:25:30.040
including concussions, compound fractures, and head injuries, 62 back.

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.560
[Dr. Ryan:] This man has a sucking wound of the chest.

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:41.580
Send him to Memorial Hospital, please.

00:25:41.580 --> 00:26:02.420
[Background sounds]

00:26:02.420 --> 00:26:05.300
[Mr. Barnes:] This test is as realistic as we can make it.

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:11.320
Realistic to the point where we can estimate just how effective we'd be in an actual crisis

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:18.120
and so the last litter-bearer can see for himself, something of his part in the whole rescue operation.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:33.180
[Traffic noises and voices]

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.340
This time it's a test.

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:38.800
Next time it may be for real.

00:26:42.660 --> 00:26:47.260
[Narrator:] In one way or another, cities across the land have awakened to this need,

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:51.280
and the key role that the emergency health service must assume.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:56.120
Sometimes it's a drill that sets off the sirens,

00:26:56.120 --> 00:26:58.800
sometimes it's a real emergency...

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:04.080
the ultimate test of how well your community has prepared for disaster.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:05.740
It's nothing new.

00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:10.920
Faced with a choice of survival or extinction, man will always choose to live.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:16.280
Sometimes the price he pays is greater; sometimes less.

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:18.980
It has never been a bargain.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:23.900
[Mr. Barnes:] The lesson we learned was that it doesn't matter where you begin;

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:26.420
the important thing is to begin.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:31.580
It took a tornado to wake us up, but in a way we were lucky.

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:34.420
It could have been far worse.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:37.220
[Narrator:] What is the price of survival for you?

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:38.300
For your city?

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:39.440
Your community?

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:41.640
How do you choose to meet it?

00:27:41.640 --> 00:27:44.480
These questions are yours to answer.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:56.540
[Music]

